WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:09.740
In this video, we'll look at how we can add content to our Unreal Engine project and then use it in our levels.

2
00:00:09.939 --> 00:00:15.939
So in order to add a feature or content pack, we can do that in a couple different places.

3
00:00:15.939 --> 00:00:21.940
One is the add button inside of the content drawer or content browser.

4
00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:30.940
Or additionally, we can click the FAB button if we would like to download and install third party content from the FAB marketplace.

5
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:34.829
So let's take a look real quick.

6
00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:39.429
Go in here, we can see our add button.

7
00:00:39.429 --> 00:00:43.500
We can import to current folder if we have assets on our computer.

8
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.500
We can add a feature or content pack, which is what we'll do.

9
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.500
In this case, we already have the third person content pack downloaded.

10
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:59.500
But we can go over here to content and add the starter content to our project.

11
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:08.500
This will come with a bunch more materials and static meshes and effects that we can add to our level to give us a good starting place.

12
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:19.500
Additionally, these different features can be added when you create your project from a template inside of the Epic Games launcher.

13
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.500
And if you want to add content from FAB, the FAB button is right here in the content browser.

14
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:33.500
And we can see that we can search in here for anything that we want and add it to our project through this window.

15
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:43.650
All right, so we're going to take a little bit of time here and dive into the starter content pack, playing with some of the assets and fleshing our level out.

16
00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:49.129
So I'm going to go look at these architecture assets.

17
00:01:49.129 --> 00:01:57.129
Pick a wall here and we can start to see a size that this blueprint that we created is a little big.

18
00:01:57.129 --> 00:01:59.129
So we can play with the scale.

19
00:01:59.129 --> 00:02:02.129
We can lock all the parameters together here.

20
00:02:02.129 --> 00:02:04.129
Maybe we'll see what this looks like.

21
00:02:04.129 --> 00:02:06.129
Set it to 0.5.

22
00:02:06.129 --> 00:02:09.129
That looks a little more reasonable for a cube.

23
00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:15.949
Bring that one down and scoot this wall back a little bit.

24
00:02:15.949 --> 00:02:26.949
We can use Alt when we click on the gizmo here to create another copy, which is a great thing when you are prototyping quickly.

25
00:02:26.949 --> 00:02:31.180
Let's see, we can drag in this wall as well.

26
00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:37.689
We can change all of our different snap settings and rotational settings up here.

27
00:02:37.689 --> 00:02:44.780
So I can go ahead and set this maybe to 90 and then press E to pull up the rotate.

28
00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.780
Snap it 90 degrees.

29
00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:52.909
Press W to pull back or to get back my translate gizmo.

30
00:02:52.909 --> 00:02:59.780
And then once again use Alt and drag off a couple more copies.

31
00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.780
And then let's grab both of these again.

32
00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:09.780
I can do shift or control click to grab those and then Alt to bring over my wall.

33
00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:15.780
I'm flying around through the level using the WASD keys and holding right click.

34
00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.780
So that allows me to reposition my camera.

35
00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.780
And I'm going to use this to drag these forward.

36
00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.509
Get lined up there.

37
00:03:26.509 --> 00:03:28.509
Looks beautiful.

38
00:03:28.509 --> 00:03:34.659
Awesome.

39
00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:37.659
Let's see if we can find some other fun assets to use.

40
00:03:37.659 --> 00:03:41.659
We're going to be adding some stuff in from our Unity scene in just a minute.

41
00:03:41.659 --> 00:03:47.270
So we won't do too much but maybe we can drag in this platform.

42
00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:51.270
And I would like to scale this up a little bit.

43
00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:54.500
Give ourselves a bigger platform to work with.

44
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.500
That looks pretty good.

45
00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:06.340
We can reset various values up here.

46
00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:11.400
And let's put it right there in the middle of our...

47
00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:15.990
Does that look right?

48
00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:20.740
Cool.

49
00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:29.860
And let's add maybe some.

50
00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:33.860
We'll look more into fighting a little bit later on.

51
00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:37.490
Let's see if we look at this.

52
00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:40.990
Looks like we might need to rotate it.

53
00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:41.990
There we go.

54
00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:47.100
Let's add one more light over here.

55
00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.420
Rotate it as well.

56
00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:57.139
Maybe one more light in the back.

57
00:04:57.139 --> 00:04:58.259
Perfect.

58
00:04:58.259 --> 00:05:02.259
We want to make sure if we look down here in the bottom right,

59
00:05:02.259 --> 00:05:07.680
we can actually see all the assets that are unsaved in our level.

60
00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:11.680
So every time you import something or change an asset,

61
00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.680
or working on a blueprint or something down here or material,

62
00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.680
it will show up in this list of unsaved assets.

63
00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.680
And if you want to save them all at the same time instead of individually,

64
00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.680
we can do that here with this list.

65
00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:28.350
So I'll save everything.

66
00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:31.480
And that looks good to go.

67
00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.620
Click on off over there.

68
00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:38.620
Sweet.

69
00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:41.620
So now when we press play, we see our spinning cubes

70
00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.910
and we see our cool.

71
00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:51.649
I think additionally, I'd like to move this cube over here.

72
00:05:51.649 --> 00:05:57.149
I'd like to move this one over here.

73
00:05:57.149 --> 00:06:01.970
Move this forward a little bit.

74
00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:02.970
Alrighty.

75
00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:05.970
In the next video, we'll look at how we can take our content from Unity

76
00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:07.970
and bring it into Unreal Engine.
